findings.fix:
Add an onRequest interceptor that reads the XSRF-TOKEN cookie on the client and attaches it as X-XSRF-TOKEN for non-safe methods. Example:

export function useApi() {
  const config = useRuntimeConfig()
  return $fetch.create({
    baseURL: config.public.apiBase,
    credentials: 'include',
    onRequest({ options }) {
      if (import.meta.client) {
        const method = (options.method ?? 'GET').toString().toUpperCase()
        if (['POST', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'].includes(method)) {
          const match = document.cookie.match(/(?:^|;\s*)XSRF-TOKEN=([^;]+)/)
          if (match) {
            const headers = new Headers(options.headers as HeadersInit | undefined)
            headers.set('X-XSRF-TOKEN', decodeURIComponent(match[1]))
            options.headers = headers
          }
        }
      }
    },
  })
}

Add a unit test that verifies the header is set for POST and not set for GET. Note: SSR-side state-changing requests are not expected from the bootstrap flow; if they ever are, you must forward the cookie + request a token first.

